mance) than raw labels, validated by ChatGPT and in-house human annotators on disagreements?\"\n\n1. OVERVIEW & HYPOTHESIS\nH1 (two-sided to falsification, tested conjunctively): On a fixed-model protocol with identical splits/hyperparameters, models trained on Docta-cleaned labels (arm C) achieve higher held-out predictive performance than models trained on raw labels (arm R), AND, separately and independently, on the raw\u2194cleaned disagreement subset, both ChatGPT and in-house human annotators each favor the cleaned label more often than the raw label. H1 is therefore a conjunctive claim with a single pre-specified verdict (see \u00a73).\n\nArms: R (train on raw labels), C (train on Docta-cleaned labels), optional reference arm A (train on dual-validator-adjudicated labels), all holding the SAME base model, hyperparameters, train/val/test partitions, seeds, and test class priors. The optional arm A is descriptive only (never the object of the headline claim), included to check whether adjudicated labels beat both R and C.\n\n2. DATASET & PREPROCESSING (defaults to be confirmed per topic)\n- N total instances of a supervised task (assumption: classification; regression variant noted in \u00a77). Split 70/10/20 train/val/test (default), identical partition object used for both arms to make every run paired. Test priors matched.\n- D = {instances where raw label \u2260 cleaned label}. If Docta drops instances (\"cleaning\" = relabel+drop), handle explicitly: model trained on Docta's emitted instance set; a parallel R\u2032 model trained on raw labels restricted to Docta's surviving instance IDs is the comparability control against a naive R-on-all-instances baseline (\u00a76). Both interpretations reported.\n- If |D| > n_D, draw a stratified random sample (default n_D = 300, \u2264400 budget cap) for validator adjudication; else use all of D.\n\n3. COMMIT 1 \u2014 PRIMARY ENDPOINT & PRE-REGISTERED DECISION RULE\nDecision: predictive performance is the PRIMARY quantitative endpoint; validator agreement is a REQUIRED co-primary corroboration. The headline claim \"cleaned labels are more credible\" is supported only if the performance leg AND each validator leg independently pass. The two validators are NEVER merged into a single \"consensus truth\"; they are three separate legs (performance, human, ChatGPT), with validator-vs-validator agreement reported only as a consistency diagnostic.\n\nPrimary performance endpoint (single metric, pre-specified): held-out macro-F1 on the identical test set, \u0394_i = macroF1(C)_i \u2212 macroF1(R)_i per run.\n\nRuns: S=5 seeds \u00d7 K=5 independent splits = 25 paired runs (each split shared by R and C; seeds vary init/data-order only).\n\nPre-registered decision rule (thresholds are defaults, locked before results are seen; they are to be re-confirmed with the concrete dataset at the calibration step, but the logic and legs are fixed):\n- PERFORMANCE LEG (P): pass iff (i) mean \u0394 \u2265 \u03b4_min = +1.0 pp macro-F1; (ii) one-sided bootstrap 95% CI lower bound on mean \u0394 > 0; (iii) paired Wilcoxon signed-rank over the 25 runs, two-sided p < 0.05.\n- HUMAN LEG (H): on the sampled D, human-majority adjudication (3 annotators, expert tie-break) sides with the cleaned label on \u2265 60% of items, with one-sided 95% Wilson lower bound > 55%, and cleaned-favoring > raw-favoring in a paired binomial test (p < 0.05) counting \"neither\" conservatively as non-clean-favoring.\n- CHATGPT LEG (G): same thresholds as H, computed from ChatGPT's independent judgments (temperature 0, fixed prompt), never informed of H's outcome.\n- VERDICT:\n  \u00b7 H1 SUPPORTED iff P \u2227 H \u2227 G all pass.\n  \u00b7 P passes but H and/or G fail \u2192 \"PARTIALLY SUPPORTED (performance evidence only, validator corroboration absent)\" \u2014 explicitly NOT counted as success.\n  \u00b7 P fails (regardless of H/G) \u2192 \"NOT SUPPORTED\" (performance is primary; validator legs alone are insufficient).\n  \u00b7 H and G strongly contradict (e.g., cohen's kappa < 0.2 on D) \u2192 force a MIXED-EVIDENCE verdict even if P\u2227H\u2227G numerically pass (pre-registered failure of the dual-validator design premise).\nEvery verdict is reported with the three legs' statistics reported separately.\n\n4. COMMIT 2 \u2014 BOTH GROUND-TRUTH SCENARIOS\nScenario (i) GOLD/HELD-OUT TEST SET EXISTS:\n- Evaluation reference = the independent gold test set (never used for training or cleaning).\n- Protocol: train R and C with identical pipeline; evaluate both on the identical gold test set. \u0394(macro-F1) is measured against gold \u2192 unbiased generalization comparison.\n- Label-level credibility check: accuracy of raw vs cleaned labels directly against gold labels on the test set (does cleaning move labels toward gold?). If gold overlaps D, raw-vs-clean on gold is the strongest \"who is right\" evidence.\n- Interpretation: strongest form of the claim; predictive performance on gold = unbiased estimate; \u0394 is the headline effect; validator legs still run on D (train-side) as the question requires, but gold is the authoritative arbiter where available.\nScenario (ii) NO GOLD TEST SET EXISTS:\n- No independent reference. Held-out test portion is still (raw- and cleaned-) labeled, but evaluating both arms on raw labels would bias toward R, and on cleaned labels would be circular toward C. Therefore:\n- The held-out test split's reference is CONSTRUCTED by the dual-validator protocol: human-majority adjudication (3 humans, expert tie-break) on the full test split gives the pseudo-gold reference; ChatGPT adjudication over the same split is computed as a parallel reference for sensitivity.\n- Metrics: same primary macro-F1 of R vs C evaluated against the human-adjudicated test reference; accuracy/AUC as secondary; label-level credibility = proximity: cleaned-vs-reference accuracy vs raw-vs-reference accuracy on held-out.\n- Interpretation (must be explicitly weaker): claims are conditional on the adjudicated reference \u2014 evidence that C generalizes at least as well to the validated portion and that cleaned labels sit closer to validator-established truth. Every report under (ii) carries the caveat \"no gold standard; reference is constructed\". The design reports results under BOTH references (human-based and ChatGPT-based) as a sensitivity table, never pooled into one number. If scenario (i) gold labels are obtainable even partially, the subset with gold is analyzed under (i) rules.\n\n5. DUAL-VALIDATOR DISAGREEMENT-RESOLUTION PROTOCOL (numbers included)\n- D sample: n_D = 300 stratified by class (cap 400; all D if smaller).\n- In-house humans: m = 3 independent annotators per item; each sees item + \"Label A\"/\"Label B\" (source-blind, order randomized/counterbalanced across items and annotators); a third distinct label may be entered. Adjudicated label = majority of 3; if no majority, senior/expert annotator (pre-defined person or a documented rule of selecting the label matching the task gold-standard description) tie-breaks.\n- ChatGPT: fixed model version, temperature 0, one seeded run, fixed prompt template mirroring the human task exactly (same source-blind A/B framing); output parsed programmatically; no human feedback loop. ChatGPT never sees human answers.\n- Coding for analysis: per item, human outcome \u2208 {favor-cleaned, favor-raw, neither}; ChatGPT outcome \u2208 {favor-cleaned, favor-raw, neither}; \"neither\" counted conservatively against cleaned (\u00a73). Consistency diagnostics: cohen's kappa (human vs ChatGPT on D), and % of D where both favor cleaned.\n- Budget note: Scenario (i) annotates n_D\u2248300; Scenario (ii) additionally adjudicates the full held-out test split (cap e.g. 400\u20131000 items) to construct the reference.\n\n6. CONTROLS\n- Baseline: arm R (raw labels) is the explicit baseline for \u0394; additionally a random-label and majority-class classifier gives floor performance for context.\n- Fixed pr